<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qalhat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qalhat is an ancient port city on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, south of Sur. From the 11th to the 15th century it was the second city of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> and a major trading port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>. An earthquake damaged it in the late 15th century, and the Portuguese sacked it in 1508. Its ruins became a World Heritage Site in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qalhat.jpg" alt="Qalhat" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: klorrainegraham · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qalhat_flickr01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
